# Reflection

One of my biggest goals in taking this class was gaining two completed projects and building them from the ground up. Along the way, my goal was to learn about concept design, and take it and other tools such as LLMs for a spin. My broader goals were to gain experience to become a SWE, design software, and think about the why, not just the how, and I think that this project represented a significant step towards that, and towards thinking big rather than at a line-by-line, function level. I am proud of the final project. I executed a clear, well-scoped idea, and my website looks and feels how I imagined it.

Once I distilled the idea, the idea itself, coming up with the concepts, and implementing the concepts was relatively simple. Coming up with the design was more difficult. For example, thinking of the colors and comparing colors that I found online was overwhelming at times. Then thinking of how to name or arrange the elements was like pulling teeth. I think the mistake I made was not thinking of the design enough in the initial ideation and design phases. I think another, general mistake I made was underestimating or overestimating the amount of time that things would take, and not giving myself enough time to brainstorm ideas. On the contrary, especially with AI, implementation took a smaller fraction of time. In the future, to avoid this mistake, I will try to have a clearer vision to begin with, and give myself more time to brainstorm and clarify ideas. Additionally, I will challenge myself on ideas.

Another goal of mine was to work with LLMs and not let them replace my learning or skill acquisition. I acquired the skill to work with an LLM, as well as write a concept design, execute it, work with a database, think critically about user interface and experience, and write in HTML/CSS. I still need to develop the skill of building around AI, as there were no AI integrations in my website, and working with a SQL rather than NoSQL database. I wrote the concept design myself, created all of the folders and files (the file structure), and described scenarios to test. Then I let an LLM write the tests and write the code against the tests. Additionally, I used an LLM to overview unfamiliar technologies, debug deploy errors, conduct systematic reviews, and draft docs that I reviewed. I still thought about what each file, class, and function was doing, and I just let LLMs generate tests and sample data within very specific parameters, or write code that had to match the concept design and pass tests. This reflects my conclusion about the appropriate role of LLMs in software development. The amount of time it took to fully specify, write, and test features that seemed simple and intuitive to me surprised me.

I wish I could have implemented more features, such as the ability to move, edit, and delete posts, literary elements, and members. Additionally, I wish I could have added more to the design, like animations or cute images.

From user testing, I learned that as a designer, it is easy to be lost in the sauce. It was shocking to see what it was like to drop someone entirely new into the design. It made me think about adding tutorial screens and pages so not so much was on one page. Additionally, in the future, I would definitely try to fully step back from my project for longer periods of time so I could see it from fresh eyes and see potential confusions after having cleared my own cache of just building it.